from international_completion_helpers import add,save
add(1976,1,'Geometry',4,'Two area bounds must both attain equality; their equality conditions determine a perpendicular configuration and hence the other diagonal.',r'A convex quadrilateral has area $32$. Two opposite sides and one diagonal have total length $16$. Determine the possible length of its other diagonal.',r'''Label the quadrilateral $ABCD$ so that $AB+CD+AC=16$, and put $AC=x$. The perpendicular heights of $B,D$ from $AC$ are at most $AB,CD$. Thus
$$32\le\frac{x(AB+CD)}2=\frac{x(16-x)}2\le32.$$
Equality forces $x=8$, $AB+CD=8$, $AB\perp AC$ and $CD\perp AC$. The horizontal and vertical components of $BD$ therefore both have length eight, giving $BD=8\sqrt2$. Conversely, any positive $AB,CD$ summing to eight in this perpendicular configuration gives a convex quadrilateral with the required area, so this value is attained.''',['Area','Equality cases','Pythagoras'])
add(1976,2,'Algebra',4.5,'The substitution x=2 cos t converts iterated squaring into angle doubling; the key remaining step is to count the distinct fixed-point angles accurately.',r'Set $P_1(x)=x^2-2$ and $P_{j+1}(x)=P_j(x)^2-2$. Prove that $P_n(x)-x$ has only real, simple roots for every positive integer $n$.',r'Let $N=2^n$. Induction using $2\cos(2t)=(2\cos t)^2-2$ gives $P_n(2\cos t)=2\cos Nt$. Thus roots arise from $t=2k\pi/(N-1)$ and $t=2k\pi/(N+1)$ in $[0,\pi]$. The first family has $N/2$ members and the second $N/2+1$. Their only common member is zero: since $\gcd(N-1,N+1)=1$, equality of the fractions forces both numerators to be zero within the specified range. Cosine is injective on $[0,\pi]$, giving exactly $N$ distinct real roots. The polynomial has degree $N$, so these are all its roots and each is simple.',['Polynomials','Trigonometry','Iteration'])
add(1976,3,'Geometry',6,'Translate optimal packing into three floor ratios, force one side to be two by a uniform bound, then use divisibility by five and sharper bounds to finish the finite classification.',r'An integer-sided rectangular box is packed with as many axis-parallel cubes of volume two as possible. Their total volume is exactly $40\%$ of the box. Determine its dimensions.',r'''Write $k=\sqrt[3]2$ and $f(n)=\lfloor n/k\rfloor$. The maximum number packed is $f(a)f(b)f(c)$: partition the possible centre coordinates into $f(a),f(b),f(c)$ intervals of length below $k$; two centres in one resulting cell would give overlapping cubes. A rectangular array attains this bound. Hence $5f(a)f(b)f(c)=abc$.

For every integer $n\ge3$, $f(n)/n\ge3/5$: check $3,4,5$, and use $f(n)>n/k-1$ for $n\ge6$. Since $(3/5)^3>1/5$, one side is two; a side one would pack nothing. The remaining ratios have product $2/5$.

One remaining side is divisible by five. For a multiple $m\ge10$, $f(m)/m\ge7/10$ (check ten; use the same bound from fifteen onwards). The other ratio would be at most $4/7<3/5$, forcing that side also to be two. This would require $f(m)/m=4/5>1/k$, impossible.

Thus one side is five. The last side $n$ satisfies $f(n)/n=2/3$, so $3\mid n$. For $n\ge9$ the lower bound exceeds $2/3$. Both $n=3,6$ work. The dimensions, up to order, are $(2,3,5)$ and $(2,5,6)$.''',['Solid geometry','Packing','Floors','Divisibility'])
add(1976,4,'Algebra',3.5,'Local replacements reduce all factors to twos and threes, after which the residue of the fixed sum determines the maximum; the standard integer-product argument is below the target band.',r'Find the largest product of a finite list of positive integers whose sum is $1976$.',r'A maximal product has no factor one: merge it into another factor. Any factor $m\ge5$ can be replaced by $3,m-3$, increasing the product. Replace every four by two twos without changing it. Three twos can be replaced by two threes, increasing the product, so at most two twos remain. The sum $1976=3\cdot658+2$ forces exactly one two and 658 threes. The largest product is therefore $2\cdot3^{658}$.',['Optimisation','Integer constraints','Local improvement'])
add(1976,5,'Combinatorics',4.5,'Count bounded integer inputs and the vectors of linear-form outputs; a collision produces the required small nonzero kernel vector.',r'A homogeneous system has $p$ equations in $q=2p$ unknowns, with all coefficients in $\{-1,0,1\}$. Prove it has a nonzero integer solution with every coordinate of absolute value at most $q$.',r'Consider every integer vector $v\in[-p,p]^{2p}$. There are $(2p+1)^{2p}=(4p^2+4p+1)^p$ such vectors. Each of the $p$ linear forms takes an integer value between $-2p^2$ and $2p^2$, so there are at most $(4p^2+1)^p$ possible output vectors. The first count is larger. By the pigeonhole principle two distinct inputs $v,w$ have equal outputs. Their difference is a nonzero integer solution, and every coordinate lies in $[-2p,2p]=[-q,q]$.',['Pigeonhole principle','Counting','Linear algebra'])
add(1976,6,'Algebra',5,'Identify a reciprocal-power closed form for the nonlinear recurrence, and verify it by two exponent identities before taking the floor.',r'Define $u_0=2$, $u_1=5/2$, and $u_{n+1}=u_n(u_{n-1}^2-2)-u_1$ for $n\ge1$. Show that $\lfloor u_n\rfloor=2^{(2^n-(-1)^n)/3}$ for every $n\ge1$.',r'''Put $e_n=(2^n-(-1)^n)/3$, a nonnegative integer, and $v_n=2^{e_n}+2^{-e_n}$. Then $v_0=2,v_1=5/2$. The identities
$$e_n+2e_{n-1}=e_{n+1},\qquad e_n-2e_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-1}$$
show, by multiplying the two reciprocal-power pairs, that
$$v_n(v_{n-1}^2-2)=v_{n+1}+2+\tfrac12.$$
Thus $v_n$ obeys the defining recurrence, and induction gives $u_n=v_n$. For $n\ge1$, $e_n\ge1$, so $2^{e_n}$ is an integer and $0<2^{-e_n}<1$. Taking floors proves the formula.''',['Recurrences','Algebraic identities','Floors'])
save()
